=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-self-93408d6231229433.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-self-93408d6231229433.png\">","<img style=\"width: 400px\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-self-685174f10d144be8.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-self-685174f10d144be8.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-self-685174f10d144be8.png\">"],"answer":3,"solution":"The given reaction is an intramolecular Cannizzaro reaction of a dialdehyde having $-CHO$ and $-CDO$ groups. <br><br> In the first step, the hydroxide ion ($OH^-$) attacks the carbonyl carbon. The $-CHO$ carbon is slightly more electrophilic than the $-CDO$ carbon due to the +I effect of deuterium. More importantly, in the rate-determining step, the transfer of a hydride ion ($H^-$) is faster than the transfer of a deuteride ion ($D^-$) due to the primary kinetic isotope effect ($k_H \\gt  k_D$). <br><br> Therefore, the $OH^-$ attacks the $-CHO$ group, forming an intermediate that transfers a hydride ion to the $-CDO$ group. As a result, the $-CHO$ group is oxidized to a carboxylic acid (which forms the carboxylate salt in basic medium), and the $-CDO$ group is reduced to an alcohol. <br><br> The intermediate product X is sodium 2-(hydroxymethyl-d1)benzoate, where the original $-CHO$ group becomes a $-COONa$ group and the original $-CDO$ group becomes a $-CH(D)OH$ group. <br><br> In the second step, treatment of X with excess HCl gas protonates the carboxylate salt to form 2-(hydroxymethyl-d1)benzoic acid. Under these acidic conditions, the molecule undergoes intramolecular esterification (lactonization), eliminating a molecule of water to form a stable five-membered lactone ring. <br><br> The final product P is a phthalide derivative, which features a carbonyl group in the lactone ring and a deuterium atom on the $sp^3$ carbon.<br><br>Answer: <img style=\"width: 400px\" src=\"https://fi